  "live_records": {
    "q-34070": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34070",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,1,3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x-6y-6z=-15.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=-1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6y-6z & =-15 \\\\ 3(1)-6(-1)-6z & =-15 \\\\ -6z & =-24 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,-1,4)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-1,4⟩-⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,-6,-6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-6)|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-3+12-6|}{\\sqrt{9+36+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
