    "q-80114": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80114",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,7,20)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $r\\cdot ⟨6,-9,3⟩=51.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a point on the plane. If $r=⟨x,y,z⟩,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-9,3⟩ & =51 \\\\ 6x-9y+3z & =51 \\\\ 3z & =51-6x+9y \\\\ z & =17-2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $x=1$ and $y=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nz=17-2(1)+3(2)=21.\n$$\n\nSo, the point $Q(1,2,21)$ lies on the plane. \n\nNow, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,2,21⟩-⟨2,7,20⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨6,-9,3⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 6+(-5)\\cdot (-9)+1\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6+45+3|}{\\sqrt{36+81+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|42|}{\\sqrt{126}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
