    "q-129160": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129160",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,1,5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $-2x+4y+4z=18.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=-1$ and $y=2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4y+4z & =18 \\\\ -2(-1)+4(2)+4z & =18 \\\\ 4z & =8 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-1,2,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,2,2⟩-⟨-2,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,4,4⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2+4-12|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
